    autonomyLede: "You never stream commands, and you never run a plan from the ground. A mission arrives as goals — desired end states — and travels over the mesh from the GC-SPU to every unit once. There's no plan to send, because the plan doesn't exist anywhere central: each unit builds and reworks its own, on-board, straight off the shared world state. The GC-SPU is just the peer that happens to introduce the goals; from there every unit picks up what it can work on and pursues it independently. Losing the link to the operator doesn't pause anything — nothing about how a unit plans ever depended on that link being up, so there's nothing to fail over to.",
    autonomyBeforeLabel: "PUSH THE MISSION · ONCE, OVER THE MESH",
    autonomyAfterLabel: 'LINK CUT · EVERY UNIT STILL PLANNING, STILL COORDINATING',
    meshTag: "MESH",
    meshTitle: "The swarm never syncs. It just keeps decoding.",
    meshLede: "Keeping a fleet's shared picture consistent usually means handshakes: ask a peer what it has, request what's missing, wait for the answer. Every one of those exchanges can be lost, and a busy or jammed channel loses them first. The SPU doesn't ask. Every unit streams a rateless code of everything it knows and decodes whatever it hears, from whoever it hears it — no requests, no acknowledgements, no sessions, so there's no exchange to lose. A unit that joins late, or comes back from behind a hill, simply decodes its way back to the current picture. Positions ride a separate latency tier that slows down gracefully when the channel is busy — 5 Hz, then 2, 1, 0.5 — instead of stalling, while the rest of the shared state keeps converging underneath.",
    meshMore: "How it works: ",
    meshMoreLink: "The swarm that never syncs",
